<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Chapter: Orthogonality and Its Applications
  MATH 53, Linear Algebra (Mehdi Ahmadi)

  Status: OUTLINE. Every section and subsection below carries a one-paragraph
  description of what it will contain; the mathematics is to be written later.

  Included in source/main.ptx as the final content chapter, after ch-applications.ptx
  and before ch-problems.ptx. (It draws on Chapters 2, 4, 5 and the dot product of
  Section 6.4, but it also refers forward to Section 7.7 and to Chapter 8, so it sits
  after them.) When the sections are written they can be split into
  source/orthogonality/sec-*.ptx and pulled in with xi:include, exactly as the other
  chapters do.

  Notation follows the rest of the book:
    vectors \vec{u}, \vec{v}, \vec{w}, \vec{b};   spaces \R^n, \R^m;
    \vec{u} \cdot \vec{v},  \| \vec{u} \|;        A^T;
    \operatorname{Col} A, \operatorname{Nul} A, \operatorname{rank} A, \operatorname{Span}\{ \dots \};
    \widehat{W} = W / \| W \| for a unit vector (as in Section 7.7);
    \vec{b}_{\parallel}, \vec{b}_{\perp} for the components along and perpendicular
    to a subspace (as in Section 7.7);  W^{\perp} for the orthogonal complement.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-orthogonality" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Orthogonality and Its Applications</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The dot product measures lengths and angles, and the most useful angle is a right
      angle. This chapter builds the theory of perpendicularity in <m>\R^n</m>: the
      orthogonal complement of a subspace, bases whose vectors are mutually perpendicular,
      and the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace <mdash /> the vector in the
      subspace that is closest to it. Along the way the transpose <m>A^T</m> finally shows
      its geometric face, the Gram<ndash />Schmidt algorithm produces orthogonal bases on
      demand, and every matrix with independent columns acquires a factorization
      <m>A = QR</m>. The payoff is the method of least squares, which extracts the best
      possible answer from a system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> that has no solution at all,
      and which is the engine behind fitting lines and curves to data.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================ -->
  <section xml:id="sec-dot-product-rn">
    <title>The Dot Product in <m>\R^n</m></title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <xref ref="def-dot-product" text="type-global" /> introduced the dot product,
        length, and angle in <m>\R^3</m> in order to measure volume. This short section
        lifts those definitions to <m>\R^n</m>, where there is no picture to lean on but
        every formula survives unchanged, and it records the handful of algebraic rules
        that the rest of the chapter uses on every page.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-dot-length-distance">
      <title>Dot Product, Length, and Distance</title>
      <p>
        Defines <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = u_1v_1 + \cdots + u_nv_n</m> for vectors in
        <m>\R^n</m>, the length <m>\| \vec{u} \| = \sqrt{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{u}}</m>, and
        the distance <m>\| \vec{u} - \vec{v} \|</m> between two vectors. Lists the
        algebraic properties (symmetry, linearity in each slot, positivity) and the
        scaling rule <m>\| c\vec{u} \| = |c| \, \| \vec{u} \|</m>, which makes
        <m>\widehat{\vec{u}} = \vec{u} / \| \vec{u} \|</m> a unit vector. Worked examples
        in <m>\R^4</m> and <m>\R^5</m> emphasize that the computation is identical in every
        dimension.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-vectors">
      <title>Orthogonal Vectors and the Pythagorean Theorem</title>
      <p>
        Declares <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> <term>orthogonal</term> when
        <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 0</m>, explains why this is the right generalization of
        <q>perpendicular</q> (the angle formula
        <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = \| \vec{u} \| \| \vec{v} \| \cos\theta</m> gives
        <m>\theta = \pi/2</m>), and proves the Pythagorean theorem
        <m>\| \vec{u} + \vec{v} \|^2 = \| \vec{u} \|^2 + \| \vec{v} \|^2</m> exactly when
        <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 0</m>. Notes that <m>\vec{0}</m> is orthogonal to
        everything, and that a vector orthogonal to a spanning set of a subspace is
        orthogonal to the whole subspace.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-dot-as-matrix-product">
      <title>The Dot Product as a Matrix Product</title>
      <p>
        Observes that <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = \vec{u}^T \vec{v}</m> when vectors are
        viewed as <m>n \times 1</m> matrices, and draws the immediate consequence that the
        components of <m>A^T\vec{x}</m> are the dot products of <m>\vec{x}</m> with the
        columns of <m>A</m>. This is the bridge between the geometric idea of
        perpendicularity and the algebra of the transpose from
        <xref ref="sec-transpose" text="type-global" />, and it is the reason <m>A^T</m>
        appears everywhere in the sections that follow.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================ -->
  <section xml:id="sec-orthogonal-complements">
    <title>Orthogonal Complements and the Matrix Transpose</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A subspace <m>W</m> of <m>\R^m</m> has a natural partner: the set of all vectors
        perpendicular to it. This section defines that partner, shows that it is a
        subspace, and identifies it concretely when <m>W</m> is a column space <mdash />
        the perpendicular vectors are precisely the null space of the transpose. The
        dimensions of a subspace and its complement add up to the dimension of the
        ambient space, a fact that rests on the rank<ndash />nullity relation of
        <xref ref="thm-rank-nullity" text="type-global" />.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-complement">
      <title>The Orthogonal Complement of a Subspace</title>
      <p>
        Defines the <term>orthogonal complement</term>
        <m>W^{\perp} = \{ \vec{v} \in \R^m : \vec{v} \cdot \vec{w} = 0 \text{ for every } \vec{w} \in W \}</m>,
        proves that <m>W^{\perp}</m> is a subspace, and works the basic examples: the
        complement of a line through the origin in <m>\R^3</m> is a plane, the complement
        of a plane is a line, and <m>(\R^m)^{\perp} = \{ \vec{0} \}</m>. Shows that to test
        membership in <m>W^{\perp}</m> it suffices to check a spanning set of <m>W</m>,
        and that <m>W \cap W^{\perp} = \{ \vec{0} \}</m>. An interactive figure lets the
        reader drag a plane in <m>\R^3</m> and watch its normal line follow.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-transpose-dot-product">
      <title>The Transpose and the Dot Product</title>
      <p>
        Establishes the identity
        <m>(A\vec{x}) \cdot \vec{y} = \vec{x} \cdot (A^T\vec{y})</m> for every
        <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, <m>\vec{x} \in \R^n</m>, and
        <m>\vec{y} \in \R^m</m>, by writing both sides as the matrix product
        <m>\vec{x}^T A^T \vec{y}</m>. This single formula says that <m>A</m> and <m>A^T</m>
        can be moved from one side of a dot product to the other, and it is the algebraic
        engine behind every result in this section. Examples verify it numerically and
        interpret it for symmetric matrices, where <m>A</m> may be moved freely.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-col-perp-nul-transpose">
      <title>The Orthogonal Complement of a Column Space</title>
      <p>
        Proves the central theorem
        <m>(\operatorname{Col} A)^{\perp} = \operatorname{Nul} A^T</m>: a vector
        <m>\vec{y}</m> is orthogonal to every column of <m>A</m> exactly when each dot
        product with a column vanishes, that is, exactly when <m>A^T\vec{y} = \vec{0}</m>.
        Consequently, given a spanning set for a subspace <m>W</m>, one finds a basis for
        <m>W^{\perp}</m> by placing the spanning vectors as columns of <m>A</m> and solving
        <m>A^T\vec{y} = \vec{0}</m> by row reduction. Worked examples compute the
        complement of a plane in <m>\R^3</m> and of a two-dimensional subspace of
        <m>\R^4</m>. The companion statement
        <m>(\operatorname{Nul} A)^{\perp} = \operatorname{Col} A^T</m> is recorded as well.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-dimension-complement">
      <title>Dimension of the Orthogonal Complement</title>
      <p>
        Shows that <m>\operatorname{rank} A = \operatorname{rank} A^T</m> (the pivot
        columns and the nonzero rows of the reduced form have the same count) and combines
        this with <xref ref="thm-rank-nullity" text="type-global" /> applied to <m>A^T</m>
        to prove <m>\dim W + \dim W^{\perp} = m</m> for every subspace <m>W</m> of
        <m>\R^m</m>. Deduces that <m>(W^{\perp})^{\perp} = W</m>, so that a subspace and
        its complement determine each other, and previews the decomposition
        <m>\vec{b} = \vec{b}_{\parallel} + \vec{b}_{\perp}</m> that the next section makes
        precise.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================ -->
  <section xml:id="sec-orthogonal-bases-projections">
    <title>Orthogonal Bases and Projections</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A basis whose vectors are mutually perpendicular is the best basis one can hope
        for: coordinates are read off with dot products instead of row reduction, and the
        closest point of a subspace to a given vector is written down by a formula. This
        section develops orthogonal and orthonormal sets, the projection formula, the
        projection matrix <m>P = QQ^T</m>, and the square matrices <m>Q</m> with
        <m>Q^{-1} = Q^T</m> that preserve all lengths and angles.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-sets">
      <title>Orthogonal and Orthonormal Sets</title>
      <p>
        Defines an <term>orthogonal set</term> as a set of nonzero vectors, each
        orthogonal to the others, and an <term>orthonormal set</term> as an orthogonal set
        of unit vectors. Proves the two facts that make such sets valuable: an orthogonal
        set is automatically linearly independent, and if
        <m>\vec{b} = c_1\vec{w}_1 + \cdots + c_n\vec{w}_n</m> with
        <m>\vec{w}_1, \ldots, \vec{w}_n</m> orthogonal, then the coefficients are
        <m>c_i = \dfrac{\vec{b} \cdot \vec{w}_i}{\vec{w}_i \cdot \vec{w}_i}</m>, so that
        <md>
          \vec{b} = \frac{\vec{b} \cdot \vec{w}_1}{\vec{w}_1 \cdot \vec{w}_1}\vec{w}_1
                  + \frac{\vec{b} \cdot \vec{w}_2}{\vec{w}_2 \cdot \vec{w}_2}\vec{w}_2
                  + \cdots
                  + \frac{\vec{b} \cdot \vec{w}_n}{\vec{w}_n \cdot \vec{w}_n}\vec{w}_n.
        </md>
        For an orthonormal set the denominators disappear. Examples contrast finding
        coordinates by row reduction with finding them by dot products.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthonormal-columns">
      <title>Matrices with Orthonormal Columns</title>
      <p>
        Packages an orthonormal set <m>\vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_n</m> in <m>\R^m</m> as
        the columns of an <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>Q</m> and proves
        <m>Q^TQ = I_n</m>: the <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>Q^TQ</m> is
        <m>\vec{u}_i \cdot \vec{u}_j</m>, which is <m>1</m> on the diagonal and <m>0</m>
        elsewhere. Shows the consequences <m>\| Q\vec{x} \| = \| \vec{x} \|</m> and
        <m>(Q\vec{x}) \cdot (Q\vec{y}) = \vec{x} \cdot \vec{y}</m>, so multiplication by
        <m>Q</m> preserves lengths and angles, and points out that <m>QQ^T</m> is
        generally <em>not</em> the identity when <m>Q</m> is not square.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-projection">
      <title>Orthogonal Projection onto a Subspace</title>
      <p>
        Defines the <term>orthogonal projection</term> of <m>\vec{b}</m> onto a subspace
        <m>W</m>, written <m>\vec{b}_{\parallel}</m> (or
        <m>\operatorname{proj}_W \vec{b}</m>), as the vector in <m>W</m> closest to
        <m>\vec{b}</m>, and shows it is characterized by
        <m>\vec{b} - \vec{b}_{\parallel} \in W^{\perp}</m>. Proves the
        <term>projection formula</term>: if <m>\vec{w}_1, \ldots, \vec{w}_n</m> is an
        orthogonal basis for <m>W</m>, then
        <md>
          \vec{b}_{\parallel}
          = \frac{\vec{b} \cdot \vec{w}_1}{\vec{w}_1 \cdot \vec{w}_1}\vec{w}_1
          + \cdots
          + \frac{\vec{b} \cdot \vec{w}_n}{\vec{w}_n \cdot \vec{w}_n}\vec{w}_n,
        </md>
        the same expression as the coordinate formula, now applied to a vector that need
        not lie in <m>W</m>. Begins with projection onto a line, which recovers the
        <m>X_{\parallel} = (X \cdot \widehat{W}) \, \widehat{W}</m> of
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-perpendicular" text="type-global" />, and includes
        an interactive figure showing <m>\vec{b}</m>, a plane <m>W</m>, and the foot of the
        perpendicular.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-projection-matrix">
      <title>The Projection Matrix <m>P = QQ^T</m></title>
      <p>
        Shows that orthogonal projection onto <m>W</m> is a linear transformation of
        <m>\R^m</m> and finds its matrix: if <m>\vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_n</m> is an
        orthonormal basis for <m>W</m> and
        <m>Q = [\, \vec{u}_1 \mid \cdots \mid \vec{u}_n \,]</m>, then
        <m>\vec{b}_{\parallel} = QQ^T\vec{b}</m>, so <m>P = QQ^T</m>. Verifies the
        hallmarks of a projection, <m>P^2 = P</m> and <m>P^T = P</m>, identifies
        <m>\operatorname{Col} P = W</m> and <m>\operatorname{Nul} P = W^{\perp}</m>, and
        revisits the projections of <xref ref="subsec-shear-projection" text="type-global" />
        as the special case of coordinate planes.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-decomposition">
      <title>The Orthogonal Decomposition <m>\vec{b} = \vec{b}_{\parallel} + \vec{b}_{\perp}</m></title>
      <p>
        Proves that every <m>\vec{b} \in \R^m</m> can be written in exactly one way as
        <m>\vec{b} = \vec{b}_{\parallel} + \vec{b}_{\perp}</m> with
        <m>\vec{b}_{\parallel} \in W</m> and <m>\vec{b}_{\perp} \in W^{\perp}</m>, where
        <m>\vec{b}_{\parallel}</m> is the projection onto <m>W</m> and
        <m>\vec{b}_{\perp} = \vec{b} - \vec{b}_{\parallel}</m> is the projection onto
        <m>W^{\perp}</m>. Derives the Pythagorean relation
        <m>\| \vec{b} \|^2 = \| \vec{b}_{\parallel} \|^2 + \| \vec{b}_{\perp} \|^2</m>,
        shows that the distance from <m>\vec{b}</m> to <m>W</m> is
        <m>\| \vec{b}_{\perp} \|</m>, and gives the matrix version
        <m>I = P + (I - P)</m>, where <m>I - P</m> projects onto <m>W^{\perp}</m>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-matrices">
      <title>Orthogonal Matrices</title>
      <p>
        Defines an <term>orthogonal matrix</term> as a square <m>m \times m</m> matrix
        <m>Q</m> whose columns form an orthonormal basis for <m>\R^m</m>, and proves that
        such a <m>Q</m> is invertible with <m>Q^{-1} = Q^T</m>, so that
        <m>QQ^T = Q^TQ = I</m> and the rows of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal too. Shows that
        <m>Q</m> preserves lengths, distances, and angles, and that
        <m>\det Q = \pm 1</m>. Identifies the rotations and reflections of
        <xref ref="sec-special-transformations" text="type-global" /> as exactly the
        <m>2 \times 2</m> orthogonal matrices, and notes that products and inverses of
        orthogonal matrices are orthogonal.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================ -->
  <section xml:id="sec-gram-schmidt">
    <title>Finding Orthogonal Bases</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The projection formula needs an orthogonal basis, and most bases are not
        orthogonal. The Gram<ndash />Schmidt algorithm repairs this: starting from any
        basis of a subspace it subtracts off projections one vector at a time and leaves
        an orthogonal basis behind. Recording the algorithm in matrix form produces the
        <m>QR</m> factorization, the orthogonal counterpart of the elementary-matrix
        factorization of <xref ref="sec-elementary-matrices" text="type-global" />.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-gram-schmidt-algorithm">
      <title>The Gram<ndash />Schmidt Algorithm</title>
      <p>
        Starts with two vectors in the plane, replaces the second by its component
        perpendicular to the first, and then states the general algorithm: given a basis
        <m>\vec{v}_1, \ldots, \vec{v}_n</m> for a subspace <m>W</m> of <m>\R^m</m>, set
        <md>
          <mrow>\vec{w}_1 \amp = \vec{v}_1,</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec{w}_2 \amp = \vec{v}_2
            - \frac{\vec{v}_2 \cdot \vec{w}_1}{\vec{w}_1 \cdot \vec{w}_1}\vec{w}_1,</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec{w}_3 \amp = \vec{v}_3
            - \frac{\vec{v}_3 \cdot \vec{w}_1}{\vec{w}_1 \cdot \vec{w}_1}\vec{w}_1
            - \frac{\vec{v}_3 \cdot \vec{w}_2}{\vec{w}_2 \cdot \vec{w}_2}\vec{w}_2,</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \;\; \vdots</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec{w}_n \amp = \vec{v}_n
            - \frac{\vec{v}_n \cdot \vec{w}_1}{\vec{w}_1 \cdot \vec{w}_1}\vec{w}_1
            - \cdots
            - \frac{\vec{v}_n \cdot \vec{w}_{n-1}}{\vec{w}_{n-1} \cdot \vec{w}_{n-1}}\vec{w}_{n-1}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Each <m>\vec{w}_k</m> is <m>\vec{v}_k</m> minus its projection onto
        <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{w}_1, \ldots, \vec{w}_{k-1} \}</m>, so the
        <m>\vec{w}_k</m> are orthogonal, nonzero, and span the same subspace as the
        <m>\vec{v}_k</m>. Fully worked examples in <m>\R^3</m> and <m>\R^4</m>, plus an
        animation that shows each subtraction geometrically.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthonormal-from-orthogonal">
      <title>From Orthogonal to Orthonormal</title>
      <p>
        Normalizes the output of Gram<ndash />Schmidt by setting
        <m>\vec{u}_j = \vec{w}_j / \| \vec{w}_j \|</m>, discusses when it is worth doing
        (it costs square roots but simplifies every later formula, since
        <m>\vec{w}_j \cdot \vec{w}_j</m> becomes <m>1</m>), and shows how to extend an
        orthonormal basis of a subspace <m>W</m> to an orthonormal basis of all of
        <m>\R^m</m> by running the algorithm on a basis of <m>W^{\perp}</m>. Includes a
        short Sage cell that carries out Gram<ndash />Schmidt so that hand computations can
        be checked.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-qr-factorization">
      <title>The <m>QR</m> Factorization</title>
      <p>
        Rewrites the Gram<ndash />Schmidt equations to express each original vector in
        terms of the orthonormal ones, <m>\vec{v}_k = r_{1k}\vec{u}_1 + \cdots + r_{kk}\vec{u}_k</m>
        with <m>r_{jk} = \vec{v}_k \cdot \vec{u}_j</m>, and reads the result as a matrix
        equation: if the columns of the <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> are linearly
        independent, then <m>A = QR</m>, where the columns of <m>Q</m> form an orthonormal
        basis for <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m> and <m>R = Q^TA</m> is <m>n \times n</m>,
        upper triangular, and invertible with positive diagonal entries. Worked examples
        compute <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> by hand for <m>3 \times 2</m> and <m>3 \times 3</m>
        matrices, and a remark explains why numerical software prefers <m>QR</m> to
        row reduction.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================ -->
  <section xml:id="sec-least-squares">
    <title>Orthogonal Least Squares</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> with more equations than unknowns is usually
        inconsistent: the measured <m>\vec{b}</m> does not lie in
        <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m>. The method of least squares asks instead for the
        <m>\vec{x}</m> that makes <m>A\vec{x}</m> as close to <m>\vec{b}</m> as possible,
        and orthogonal projection answers the question completely. This section derives
        the normal equations, solves them efficiently with the <m>QR</m> factorization,
        and applies the method to the problem that motivates it in practice: fitting a
        line or curve to data.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-least-squares-problem">
      <title>Inconsistent Systems and the Least-Squares Problem</title>
      <p>
        Introduces the setting with a small overdetermined system that has no solution,
        and defines a <term>least-squares solution</term> as a vector <m>\hat{\vec{x}}</m>
        minimizing <m>\| \vec{b} - A\vec{x} \|</m> over all <m>\vec{x} \in \R^n</m>.
        Since <m>A\vec{x}</m> ranges over <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m>, the closest point is
        the projection <m>\vec{b}_{\parallel}</m> of <m>\vec{b}</m> onto
        <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m>, and a least-squares solution is any
        <m>\hat{\vec{x}}</m> with <m>A\hat{\vec{x}} = \vec{b}_{\parallel}</m>. The
        minimal error is <m>\| \vec{b}_{\perp} \|</m>. An interactive figure shows
        <m>\vec{b}</m> hovering above the plane <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m> in <m>\R^3</m>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-normal-equations">
      <title>The Normal Equations</title>
      <p>
        Uses <m>(\operatorname{Col} A)^{\perp} = \operatorname{Nul} A^T</m> to convert
        <m>\vec{b} - A\hat{\vec{x}} \in (\operatorname{Col} A)^{\perp}</m> into the
        <term>normal equations</term> <m>A^TA\,\hat{\vec{x}} = A^T\vec{b}</m>, a square
        system that is always consistent. Proves that <m>A^TA</m> is invertible exactly
        when the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, in which case the
        least-squares solution is unique,
        <m>\hat{\vec{x}} = (A^TA)^{-1}A^T\vec{b}</m>, and the projection matrix onto
        <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m> is <m>P = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T</m>. Worked examples solve
        the normal equations by row reduction and check that the residual
        <m>\vec{b} - A\hat{\vec{x}}</m> is orthogonal to every column of <m>A</m>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-least-squares-qr">
      <title>Least Squares via the <m>QR</m> Factorization</title>
      <p>
        Substitutes <m>A = QR</m> into the normal equations and uses <m>Q^TQ = I</m> to
        reduce them to <m>R\hat{\vec{x}} = Q^T\vec{b}</m>, an upper triangular system
        solved by back substitution with no matrix inverse in sight. Compares the two
        routes on the same example, and notes that when the columns of <m>A</m> are
        orthonormal already the answer is simply <m>\hat{\vec{x}} = A^T\vec{b}</m>,
        which is the projection formula in disguise.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-fitting-lines">
      <title>Fitting a Line to Data</title>
      <p>
        Given data points <m>(t_1, y_1), \ldots, (t_m, y_m)</m>, seeks the line
        <m>y = c_0 + c_1 t</m> that best fits them and shows this is the least-squares
        problem <m>A\vec{c} = \vec{y}</m> with
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp t_1 \\ \vdots \amp \vdots \\ 1 \amp t_m \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        Explains what <q>best</q> means (minimizing the sum of squared vertical
        residuals), works a complete example by hand, and provides a Sage cell that fits
        and plots a line through a real data set. Interprets the residual vector and the
        quantity <m>\| \vec{y}_{\perp} \|</m> as a measure of how well the line explains
        the data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-fitting-curves">
      <title>Beyond Lines: Fitting Curves and Surfaces</title>
      <p>
        Observes that nothing in the previous subsection used the fact that the model was
        a line: any model that is linear in its unknown coefficients <mdash /> a
        quadratic <m>y = c_0 + c_1 t + c_2 t^2</m>, a sum of sines and cosines, or a
        function of several input variables <mdash /> leads to a least-squares problem
        with a different design matrix <m>A</m>. Fits a parabola and a periodic model to
        sample data, connects the polynomial case back to the interpolation problem of
        <xref ref="sec-polynomial-interpolation" text="type-global" /> (exact fit when
        there are as many parameters as data points, least squares when there are fewer),
        and closes with a caution about overfitting.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================ -->
  <section xml:id="sec-orthogonality-applications">
    <title>Further Applications of Orthogonality</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The chapter ends with three short applications in the spirit of the image
        chapters of the book. Each one takes a single idea from the chapter <mdash />
        projection, orthonormal bases, or orthogonal matrices <mdash /> and shows it
        doing real work.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-application-recognition">
      <title>Projection and the Nearest Picture</title>
      <p>
        Returns to the image-recognition setting of
        <xref ref="sec-recognition" text="type-global" />, where a picture was split into
        <m>X_{\parallel}</m> and <m>X_{\perp}</m> relative to a single weight picture
        <m>W</m>, and upgrades the single direction to a subspace spanned by several
        reference pictures. The projection formula then produces the closest combination
        of references to a new photograph, and <m>\| X_{\perp} \|</m> measures how far the
        photograph is from anything the references can explain. Gram<ndash />Schmidt on
        the references turns this into a computation with a handful of dot products.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-application-rigid-motions">
      <title>Orthogonal Matrices as Rigid Motions</title>
      <p>
        Shows that the linear transformations of <m>\R^n</m> preserving all distances are
        exactly those given by orthogonal matrices, so that rotations and reflections in
        every dimension are described by the single equation <m>Q^TQ = I</m>. Works out
        the rotation matrices of <m>\R^3</m> about the coordinate axes, uses
        <m>\det Q = \pm 1</m> to separate rotations from reflections, and illustrates with
        a short Sage animation of a rotating object whose shape never distorts.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-application-symmetric-preview">
      <title>A Preview: Symmetric Matrices Have Orthogonal Eigenvectors</title>
      <p>
        Uses the identity <m>(A\vec{x}) \cdot \vec{y} = \vec{x} \cdot (A^T\vec{y})</m> to
        prove that eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix belonging to distinct eigenvalues
        are orthogonal, and states without proof that every symmetric matrix can be
        written as <m>A = QDQ^T</m> with <m>Q</m> orthogonal and <m>D</m> diagonal. This
        is the point where orthogonality meets the eigenvalue theory of
        <xref ref="ch-eigenvalues" text="type-global" />, and it is the foundation of the
        singular value decomposition and of principal component analysis, which are left
        for a later course.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

</chapter>
